WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.160
- [Narrator] Right now,
paleontologists discover

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.660
a new species of dinosaur
every week, and it's no wonder.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.726
Dinosaurs dominated the
planet for 135 million years,

00:00:10.726 --> 00:00:13.133
but they didn't start out on top.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.230
The first dinosaurs emerged
around 230 million years ago,

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.310
during the Triassic Period.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.900
Back then, Earth looked very different.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.330
Instead of seven continents,
there was just one, Pangaea.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.420
Even early dinosaurs

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.940
were nothing like those Hollywood giants.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:39.080
Eoraptor, possibly the first
true dinosaur, was puny,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.660
especially compared to
top predators of the day,

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.410
like some of the giant Rauisuchians.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.700
Over the next 30 million years,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.900
new species of dinosaurs
and amphibians developed,

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.090
along with the first mammals.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.670
Life was flourishing.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:58.670
Then, 201 million years
ago, everything changed.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.650
Pangaea began to tear apart.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.860
The shifting tectonic plates
sparked volcanic eruptions,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.330
which, over tens of thousands of years,

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.600
tripled the amount of greenhouse
gasses in the atmosphere,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.180
and warmed the planet by
three to four degrees Celsius.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.340
It vanquished at least 30%
of all life in the process.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.460
But for some reason, maybe luck,
or some helpful adaptation,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.080
the dinosaurs sailed on through

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.720
to the dawn of the Jurassic Period,

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.380
and over the next 135 million years,

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.220
they continued to spread,
diversify, and evolve

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.300
into some familiar faces.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.240
For example, the first Tyrannosaurus

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.600
sprouted up some time around
170 million years ago.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.140
But don't get your hopes up.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.340
These guys were no T-Rex.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.690
The real sovereign of the
era was the Allosaurus.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.573
It dined on sauropods with
its meat cleaver teeth.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.820
Meanwhile, Pangaea
continued to pull apart.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.040
By 145 million years ago,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.480
it began to resemble what we see today,

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.010
forming isolated pockets

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.640
where dinosaurs grew
increasingly different.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.110
It was the heyday of dinosaur diversity,

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.270
and a new period, the Cretaceous.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.150
In Argentina, giants like Petagotitan,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.340
the biggest dinosaur on record,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.410
turned up around 100 million years ago.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.280
But the most frightening of
all appeared in the western US,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.760
around 68 million years ago.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.310
T-Rex, the largest carnivore in history.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.046
On its own, it was a formidable predator.

00:02:30.046 --> 00:02:34.090
But it turns out the T-Rex
likely hunted in packs,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.160
and where T-Rex did not roam,

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.370
other pack hunters filled the niche.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.400
In Asia, the small yet
cunning Velociraptor roamed.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.120
It was a bad time to be a mammal.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.620
And then, 66 million years ago,

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.720
the Chicxulub asteroid
wiped out all the dinosaurs.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.510
Well, almost all of them.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.140
One group remained.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.800
Their descendants might be
sitting outside your window

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.220
right now.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.180
Birds.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.852
That's right, modern birds are dinosaurs.

00:03:03.852 --> 00:03:08.852
Turns out the legacy of these
titans today is, well, dinner.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.110
This video is made in large part

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:13.540
thanks to Steve Brusatte,

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.290
and the information in his new book,

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.370
The Rise and Fall of the Dinosaurs.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.410
What interesting dinosaur facts
do you want to know about?

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:21.810
Let us know in the comments.

